1
00:04:17,882 --> 00:04:19,592
<i>Welcome to Gattaca.</i>

2
00:05:19,903 --> 00:05:23,615
You keep your workstation
so clean, Jerome.

3
00:05:24,574 --> 00:05:25,992
It's next to godliness.

4
00:05:26,576 --> 00:05:29,496
- Isn't that what they say?
- Godliness.

5
00:05:30,205 --> 00:05:35,084
I reviewed your flight plan.
Not one error in a million keystrokes.

6
00:05:35,168 --> 00:05:40,423
Phenomenal. It's right that someone
like you is taking us to Titan.

7
00:05:41,049 --> 00:05:45,094
Has the committee approved the mission?
There's been talk of delay.

8
00:05:45,428 --> 00:05:49,265
You shouldn't listen to talk.
You leave in a week.

9
00:05:50,475 --> 00:05:52,352
You've got a substance test.

10
00:06:23,383 --> 00:06:26,510
<i>If you have been notified
of a substance analysis,</i>

11
00:06:26,594 --> 00:06:29,097
<i>please report to the testing center.</i>

12
00:06:37,313 --> 00:06:40,525
Jerome. Never shy. Pisses on command.

13
00:06:42,151 --> 00:06:44,570
A beautiful piece of equipment.
Have I ever told you that?

14
00:06:44,654 --> 00:06:46,239
Only every time I'm in here.

15
00:06:46,864 --> 00:06:51,369
Occupational hazard. I see a great many
in the course of any given day.

16
00:06:51,911 --> 00:06:54,497
Yours just happens to be
an exceptional example.

17
00:06:55,915 --> 00:06:58,765
Don't know why my folks
didn't order one like that for me.

18
00:07:14,517 --> 00:07:18,313
By the way,
have I ever told you about my son?

19
00:07:18,813 --> 00:07:19,813
No, you haven't.

20
00:07:19,897 --> 00:07:21,482
Remind me to sometime.

21
00:07:23,693 --> 00:07:27,488
So, you're about to go up. One week left.

22
00:07:29,032 --> 00:07:31,182
Please tell me
you're the least bit excited.

23
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:36,304
I'll tell you at the end of the week.

24
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:50,470
Congratulations, Jerome.

25
00:07:53,556 --> 00:07:54,599
Thank you.

26
00:07:58,061 --> 00:08:01,314
How many launches are there in a day?
A dozen?

27
00:08:03,358 --> 00:08:04,609
Sometimes more.

28
00:08:06,611 --> 00:08:09,061
You're the only one
who watches every one of them.

29
00:08:13,368 --> 00:08:17,205
If you're going to pretend
like you don't care, don't look up.

30
00:08:20,124 --> 00:08:22,210
<i>The most unremarkable of events.</i>

31
00:08:23,086 --> 00:08:27,089
<i>Jerome Morrow, Navigator 1st Class,
is about to embark</i>

32
00:08:27,173 --> 00:08:32,178
<i>on a one-year manned mission to Titan,
the 14th moon of Saturn.</i>

33
00:08:33,429 --> 00:08:36,056
<i>A highly prestigious assignment.
Although, for Jerome,</i>

34
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:38,893
<i>selection was virtually
guaranteed at birth.</i>

35
00:08:40,687 --> 00:08:43,939
<i>He's blessed with all the gifts
required for such an undertaking.</i>

36
00:08:44,023 --> 00:08:46,693
<i>A genetic quotient second to none.</i>

37
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:52,448
<i>Know there is truly nothing remarkable
about the progress of Jerome Morrow.</i>

38
00:08:53,491 --> 00:08:56,577
<i>Except that I am not Jerome Morrow.</i>

39
00:09:05,628 --> 00:09:09,841
<i>I was conceived in the Riviera.
Not the French Riviera.</i>

40
00:09:10,758 --> 00:09:12,176
<i>The Detroit variety.</i>

41
00:09:14,137 --> 00:09:16,472
<i>They used to say
that a child conceived in love</i>

42
00:09:16,556 --> 00:09:18,307
<i>has a greater chance of happiness.</i>

43
00:09:20,017 --> 00:09:21,728
<i>They don't say that anymore.</i>

44
00:09:24,939 --> 00:09:28,817
<i>I'll never understand what possessed
my mother to put her faith in God's hands</i>

45
00:09:28,901 --> 00:09:31,195
<i>rather than those of her local geneticist.</i>

46
00:09:34,657 --> 00:09:39,120
<i>Ten fingers, ten toes,
that's all that used to matter. Not now.</i>

47
00:09:39,787 --> 00:09:45,251
<i>Now, only seconds old, the exact time
and cause of my death was already known.</i>

48
00:09:50,548 --> 00:09:53,342
Neurological condition,
60 percent probability.

49
00:09:53,426 --> 00:09:56,220
Manic depression, 42 percent probability.

50
00:09:56,804 --> 00:10:01,225
Attention deficit disorder,
89 percent probability. Heart disorder...

51
00:10:03,519 --> 00:10:05,313
99 percent probability.

52
00:10:05,646 --> 00:10:07,148
Early fatal potential.

53
00:10:07,732 --> 00:10:13,654
- Life expectancy: 30.2 years.
- Thirty years?

54
00:10:15,323 --> 00:10:17,241
- The name for the certificate?
- Anton.

55
00:10:17,325 --> 00:10:20,161
No. Vincent Anton.

56
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:25,333
Yeah. That's a good name.

57
00:10:29,086 --> 00:10:32,548
I know he'll do something.
You'll do something.

58
00:10:36,511 --> 00:10:37,678
Vincent?

59
00:10:38,304 --> 00:10:41,223
<i>From an early age, I came to think
of myself as others thought of me:</i>

60
00:10:41,307 --> 00:10:43,267
- Vincent!
<i>- Chronically ill.</i>

61
00:10:43,351 --> 00:10:47,604
<i>Every skinned knee and runny nose was
treated as if it were life-threatening.</i>

62
00:10:47,688 --> 00:10:50,774
I'm sorry, the insurance won't cover it.
If he fell...

63
00:10:50,858 --> 00:10:54,808
- But I was told that everything was...
- I wish there was something I could do.

64
00:11:04,372 --> 00:11:08,584
<i>Like most other parents of their day,
they were determined that their next child</i>

65
00:11:08,668 --> 00:11:12,630
<i>would be brought into the world
in what has become the natural way.</i>

66
00:11:13,589 --> 00:11:17,093
Your extracted eggs, Marie

67
00:11:17,927 --> 00:11:22,139
have been fertilized with Antonio's sperm.

68
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:26,477
After screening, we are left, as you see,
with two healthy boys

69
00:11:26,561 --> 00:11:28,688
and two very healthy girls.

70
00:11:29,105 --> 00:11:33,067
Naturally, no critical predispositions
to any of the major inheritable diseases.

71
00:11:33,442 --> 00:11:36,392
All that remains is to select
the most compatible candidate.

72
00:11:37,446 --> 00:11:41,033
First we may as well decide on gender.
Have you given it any thought?

73
00:11:41,117 --> 00:11:46,038
We would want Vincent to have a brother,
you know, to play with.

74
00:11:46,122 --> 00:11:49,584
- Of course you would. Hello, Vincent.
- Hi.

75
00:11:50,543 --> 00:11:55,298
You have specified hazel eyes,
dark hair, and fair skin.

76
00:11:56,382 --> 00:12:00,182
I've taken the liberty of eradicating
any potentially prejudicial conditions:

77
00:12:00,511 --> 00:12:04,598
Premature baldness, myopia, alcoholism,
an addictive susceptibility,

78
00:12:04,682 --> 00:12:06,850
propensity for violence,
obesity, et cetera.

79
00:12:06,934 --> 00:12:10,604
We didn't want...
I mean, diseases, yes, but...

80
00:12:10,688 --> 00:12:15,400
Right. We were just wondering if it's good
to just leave a few things to chance.

81
00:12:15,484 --> 00:12:18,487
You want to give your child
the best possible start.

82
00:12:18,905 --> 00:12:21,448
Believe me, we have enough imperfection
built in already.

83
00:12:21,532 --> 00:12:24,160
No, your child doesn't need
any additional burdens.

84
00:12:25,411 --> 00:12:27,663
And keep in mind, this child is still you.

85
00:12:27,747 --> 00:12:29,540
Simply the best of you.

86
00:12:30,708 --> 00:12:34,629
You could conceive naturally 1,000 times
and never get such a result.

87
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:40,176
<i>That's how my brother, Anton,
came into the world.</i>

88
00:12:40,676 --> 00:12:43,721
<i>A son my father considered
worthy of his name.</i>

89
00:12:57,818 --> 00:13:00,237
What did I tell you?
You see how much he's grown?

90
00:13:15,544 --> 00:13:17,838
<i>By the time we were playing
at blood brothers,</i>

91
00:13:17,922 --> 00:13:21,550
<i>I understood there was something
very different flowing through my veins.</i>

92
00:13:22,176 --> 00:13:26,555
<i>And I'd need an awful lot more than a drop
if I was going to get anywhere.</i>

93
00:13:48,244 --> 00:13:51,955
<i>Our favorite game was Chicken.
When our parents weren't watching,</i>

94
00:13:52,039 --> 00:13:54,041
<i>we used to swim as far out as we dared.</i>

95
00:13:54,792 --> 00:13:57,753
<i>It was about who would get scared
and turn back first.</i>

96
00:14:12,727 --> 00:14:14,270
Come on, you coward!

97
00:14:16,939 --> 00:14:19,275
<i>Of course it was always me.</i>

98
00:14:19,900 --> 00:14:24,071
<i>Anton was by far the stronger swimmer,
and he had no excuse to fail.</i>

99
00:14:28,659 --> 00:14:33,622
Three hundred million,
350 million, 400 million.

100
00:14:33,706 --> 00:14:35,583
<i>Maybe it was the love of the planets,</i>

101
00:14:36,042 --> 00:14:38,669
<i>maybe it was just my growing dislike
for this one,</i>

102
00:14:39,045 --> 00:14:43,131
<i>but for as long as I can remember,
I have dreamed of going into space.</i>

103
00:14:43,215 --> 00:14:49,430
Seven hundred million, 750 million,
800 million, 850 million.

104
00:14:50,431 --> 00:14:52,331
How many astronauts are there, anyway?

105
00:14:54,226 --> 00:14:56,436
I bet I could be one if I wanted.

106
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:59,315
Hey, don't eat that. It's Pluto.

107
00:15:02,818 --> 00:15:05,737
<i>My goals didn't change a lot
in the intervening years.</i>

108
00:15:05,821 --> 00:15:08,491
- Vincent.
<i>- Much to my parents' dismay.</i>

109
00:15:10,034 --> 00:15:10,868
Vincent.

110
00:15:17,416 --> 00:15:21,337
You have to be realistic.
With a heart condition like yours...

111
00:15:21,712 --> 00:15:25,341
Mom, there's a chance there's nothing
even wrong with my heart.

112
00:15:26,258 --> 00:15:27,593
One chance in a hundred.

113
00:15:28,844 --> 00:15:31,894
- Well, I'll take it, all right?
- The trouble is, they won't.

114
00:15:32,848 --> 00:15:36,435
Listen, for God's sake,
you gotta understand something.

115
00:15:43,109 --> 00:15:46,909
The only way you'll see the inside
of a spaceship is if you were cleaning it.

116
00:15:47,530 --> 00:15:51,992
<i>My father was right. It didn't matter
how much I lied on my résumé.</i>

117
00:15:52,368 --> 00:15:54,662
<i>My real résumé was in my cells.</i>

118
00:15:55,246 --> 00:15:58,832
<i>Why should anybody invest all that money
to train me</i>

119
00:15:58,916 --> 00:16:02,711
<i>when there are a thousand other applicants
with a far cleaner profile?</i>

120
00:16:03,379 --> 00:16:07,508
<i>Of course, it's illegal to discriminate.
"Genoism," it's called.</i>

121
00:16:08,217 --> 00:16:10,803
<i>But no one takes the law seriously.</i>

122
00:16:18,144 --> 00:16:22,606
<i>If you refuse to disclose, they take
a sample from a door handle,</i>

123
00:16:23,399 --> 00:16:24,524
<i>or a handshake.</i>

124
00:16:24,608 --> 00:16:27,153
<i>Even the saliva on your application form.</i>

125
00:16:28,904 --> 00:16:32,741
<i>If in doubt, a legal drug test
can just as easily become</i>

126
00:16:32,825 --> 00:16:36,245
<i>an illegal peek
at your future in the company.</i>

127
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:48,132
You sure you want to do this?
You know you're going to lose.

128
00:16:58,893 --> 00:17:03,147
<i>It was the last time we swam together,
and out into the open sea.</i>

129
00:17:03,856 --> 00:17:06,650
<i>Like always,
knowing each stroke to the horizon</i>

130
00:17:06,734 --> 00:17:09,111
<i>was one we'd have to make
back to the shore.</i>

131
00:17:22,750 --> 00:17:25,100
<i>But something was very different
about that day.</i>

132
00:17:26,045 --> 00:17:30,174
<i>Every time Anton tried to pull away,
he found me right beside him.</i>

133
00:17:34,136 --> 00:17:37,181
<i>Until finally, the impossible happened.</i>

134
00:17:38,098 --> 00:17:40,434
Vincent! Vincent!

135
00:17:55,616 --> 00:17:57,409
<i>It was the one moment in our lives</i>

136
00:17:57,493 --> 00:18:00,328
<i>that my brother was not as strong
as he believed,</i>

137
00:18:00,412 --> 00:18:01,830
<i>and I was not as weak.</i>

138
00:18:02,706 --> 00:18:05,334
<i>It was the moment
that made everything else possible.</i>

139
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:51,838
<i>Like many others in my situation,</i>

140
00:18:51,922 --> 00:18:53,882
<i>I moved around a lot
in the next few years,</i>

141
00:18:53,966 --> 00:18:55,342
<i>getting work where I could.</i>

142
00:18:56,593 --> 00:18:59,054
<i>I must have cleaned
half the toilets in the state.</i>

143
00:19:00,723 --> 00:19:02,724
<i>I belonged to a new underclass,</i>

144
00:19:02,808 --> 00:19:06,353
<i>no longer determined by social status
or the color of your skin.</i>

145
00:19:07,479 --> 00:19:08,980
Welcome to Gattaca, gentlemen.

146
00:19:09,064 --> 00:19:13,193
<i>No, we now have discrimination
down to a science.</i>

147
00:19:13,444 --> 00:19:15,654
All right, there's your cleaning material.

148
00:19:15,738 --> 00:19:18,449
Start from the front
and clean all the way back.

149
00:19:19,283 --> 00:19:22,661
And I want to see my smiling face
on that floor.

150
00:19:32,087 --> 00:19:35,382
What about you, Your Majesty?
You dreaming of space?

151
00:19:36,467 --> 00:19:37,426
Come here.

152
00:19:42,639 --> 00:19:45,642
You can start by cleaning this space
right here.

153
00:19:48,562 --> 00:19:52,482
<i>I was never more certain
of how far away I was from my goal</i>

154
00:19:52,566 --> 00:19:54,651
<i>than when I was standing right beside it.</i>

155
00:21:07,099 --> 00:21:09,601
When you clean the glass, Vincent,
don't clean it too well.

156
00:21:09,685 --> 00:21:10,602
What do you mean?

157
00:21:11,687 --> 00:21:13,272
You might get ideas.

158
00:21:14,106 --> 00:21:15,523
Yeah, but if the glass is clean,

159
00:21:15,607 --> 00:21:18,907
it'll be easier for you to see me
when I'm on the other side of it.

160
00:22:10,537 --> 00:22:12,956
<i>For all my brave talk,
I knew it was just that.</i>

161
00:22:13,582 --> 00:22:16,168
<i>No matter how much I trained
or how much I studied,</i>

162
00:22:16,585 --> 00:22:18,837
<i>the best test score in the world
wasn't gonna matter</i>

163
00:22:18,921 --> 00:22:21,021
<i>unless I had the blood test to go with it.</i>

164
00:22:22,758 --> 00:22:26,345
<i>I made up my mind
to resort to more extreme measures.</i>

165
00:22:26,887 --> 00:22:28,847
<i>The man who showed up at my doorstep</i>

166
00:22:28,931 --> 00:22:31,724
<i>didn't exactly advertise
in the Yellow Pages.</i>

167
00:22:31,808 --> 00:22:32,851
Stand straight.

168
00:22:35,479 --> 00:22:38,148
- How did you hear about me?
- People.

169
00:22:39,399 --> 00:22:43,070
Any distinguishing marks?
Tattoos, scars, birth marks?

170
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:46,365
I don't think so.

171
00:22:47,783 --> 00:22:51,286
Are you serious about this?
I hope you're not wasting my time.

172
00:22:51,537 --> 00:22:56,333
- No, I'd give 100 percent.
- That'll get you halfway there.

173
00:22:58,210 --> 00:23:02,506
That's an old edition,
but I know it all by heart.

174
00:23:03,048 --> 00:23:04,216
By heart, huh?

175
00:23:07,219 --> 00:23:09,680
You realize the commitment is binding.

176
00:23:10,931 --> 00:23:12,432
You have somebody in mind?

177
00:23:33,996 --> 00:23:37,874
<i>For the genetically superior,
success is easier to attain,</i>

178
00:23:37,958 --> 00:23:40,085
<i>but is by no means guaranteed.</i>

179
00:23:40,711 --> 00:23:43,672
<i>After all, there is no gene for fate.</i>

180
00:23:44,423 --> 00:23:46,716
<i>And when, for one reason or another,</i>

181
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:49,802
<i>a member of the elite
falls on hard times,</i>

182
00:23:49,886 --> 00:23:53,932
<i>their genetic identity becomes
a valued commodity for the unscrupulous.</i>

183
00:23:54,516 --> 00:23:57,060
<i>One man's loss is another man's gain.</i>

184
00:23:57,311 --> 00:24:01,356
His credentials are impeccable.
An expiration date you wouldn't believe.

185
00:24:07,029 --> 00:24:11,491
The guy's practically gonna live forever.
He's got an IQ off the register,

186
00:24:12,284 --> 00:24:16,371
better than 20/20 in both eyes,
and the heart of an ox.

187
00:24:16,455 --> 00:24:18,165
He could run through a wall.

188
00:24:23,420 --> 00:24:24,630
If he could still run.

189
00:24:27,341 --> 00:24:30,719
Actually, he was a big time swimming star.

190
00:24:31,386 --> 00:24:36,933
Vincent, you could go anywhere with
this guy's helix tucked under your arm.

191
00:24:41,063 --> 00:24:44,775
What did I tell you? You look so right
together, I wanna double my fee.

192
00:24:45,359 --> 00:24:46,651
We don't look anything alike.

193
00:24:46,735 --> 00:24:50,029
It's close enough. When was the last time
anybody looked at a photograph?

194
00:24:50,113 --> 00:24:52,963
You could have my face on your nametag,
for Christ's sake.

195
00:24:53,116 --> 00:24:56,466
- Well, how do I explain the accident?
- That's the beautiful thing.

196
00:24:57,037 --> 00:24:58,705
It happened out of the country.

197
00:24:58,789 --> 00:25:02,375
There's no record he ever broke his back.
As far as anyone's concerned,

198
00:25:02,459 --> 00:25:05,878
he's still a walking, talking,
fully productive member of society.

199
00:25:05,962 --> 00:25:09,383
You just have to get him clean
and fill in the last year of his life.

200
00:25:10,092 --> 00:25:11,134
You're a foreigner.

201
00:25:12,052 --> 00:25:14,888
They don't care where you were born,
just how.

202
00:25:15,514 --> 00:25:17,224
Blood has no nationality.

203
00:25:18,225 --> 00:25:20,351
As long as it's got
what they're looking for,

204
00:25:20,435 --> 00:25:22,035
it's the only passport you need.

205
00:25:23,897 --> 00:25:24,856
Who lives up there?

206
00:25:26,233 --> 00:25:27,901
Well, I certainly don't.

207
00:25:30,737 --> 00:25:33,490
<i>So began the process of becoming Jerome.</i>

208
00:25:34,199 --> 00:25:38,495
<i>Myopia is one of the most obvious signs
of a disadvantaged birth.</i>

209
00:25:38,745 --> 00:25:42,040
- Isn't there another way?
- Well, surgery's no good.

210
00:25:42,124 --> 00:25:43,709
They'll see the scars.

211
00:25:44,626 --> 00:25:46,628
It's contact lenses or a white cane.

212
00:25:48,171 --> 00:25:52,801
- Besides, the color doesn't match.
- He's right.

213
00:25:53,468 --> 00:25:54,845
My eyes are prettier.

214
00:25:58,265 --> 00:26:01,225
You understand I take 25 percent
of everything you make.

215
00:26:01,309 --> 00:26:03,186
If for some reason it doesn't work out,

216
00:26:03,270 --> 00:26:05,939
all this equipment must be returned
within seven days.

217
00:26:06,690 --> 00:26:08,983
- And you don't get your deposit back.
- Wait, wait.

218
00:26:09,067 --> 00:26:10,985
I thought you said 20 percent.

219
00:26:11,069 --> 00:26:14,281
- You told me 20 percent.
- It's not negotiable.

220
00:26:29,880 --> 00:26:30,839
I guess that's it.

221
00:26:31,590 --> 00:26:33,759
- Not quite.
- Why? What?

222
00:26:34,134 --> 00:26:36,084
There's still the matter of the height.

223
00:26:36,636 --> 00:26:39,097
- Why, how tall are you?
- Four-foot-six.

224
00:26:39,639 --> 00:26:41,265
How tall were you before the accident?

225
00:26:41,349 --> 00:26:43,977
Unfortunately his profile says he's 6'1".

226
00:26:46,021 --> 00:26:47,272
So, I can wear lifts.

227
00:26:48,857 --> 00:26:51,193
Even with lifts, you're not that tall.

228
00:26:54,279 --> 00:26:55,280
So, what?

229
00:27:01,787 --> 00:27:02,746
No.

230
00:27:03,997 --> 00:27:04,831
No.

231
00:27:07,209 --> 00:27:11,337
- No, we never discussed that.
- Oh, I thought you were serious, Vincent.

232
00:27:11,421 --> 00:27:13,089
I am serious. I'm not doing that.

233
00:27:19,930 --> 00:27:20,931
I won't do that.

234
00:27:27,395 --> 00:27:29,606
<i>Jerome never questioned
my commitment again.</i>

235
00:27:30,273 --> 00:27:32,817
<i>I took my mind off the pain
by reminding myself</i>

236
00:27:32,901 --> 00:27:35,069
<i>that when I eventually did stand up,</i>

237
00:27:35,153 --> 00:27:38,323
<i>I'd be exactly two inches
closer to the stars.</i>

238
00:27:39,074 --> 00:27:42,160
- Are you all right?
- Yeah.

239
00:27:45,914 --> 00:27:47,249
Do you want to go dancing?

240
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:51,378
You had to be a right-hander, huh?

241
00:27:52,462 --> 00:27:54,631
I guess no one orders southpaws anymore.

242
00:27:56,675 --> 00:27:57,676
"Jerome Morrow."

243
00:27:59,761 --> 00:28:00,929
It's a nice name.

244
00:28:04,099 --> 00:28:07,561
- It's my name.
- I can't be you without it.

245
00:28:08,061 --> 00:28:10,161
What makes you think you can be me at all?

246
00:28:19,656 --> 00:28:20,657
Look at this.

247
00:28:22,951 --> 00:28:23,785
Look at it.

248
00:28:25,370 --> 00:28:26,329
It's nice.

249
00:28:28,540 --> 00:28:30,041
I'm impressed. Is it real?

250
00:28:31,376 --> 00:28:33,169
Are you colorblind too, Vincent?

251
00:28:33,962 --> 00:28:35,880
- It's silver.
- So?

252
00:28:35,964 --> 00:28:39,843
Jerome Morrow was never meant
to be one step down on the podium.

253
00:28:41,595 --> 00:28:43,138
With all I had going for me...

254
00:28:45,140 --> 00:28:46,516
I was still second best.

255
00:28:49,185 --> 00:28:50,020
Me.

256
00:28:52,355 --> 00:28:54,482
So, how do you expect to pull this off?

257
00:28:57,027 --> 00:28:58,403
I don't know, exactly.

258
00:29:04,242 --> 00:29:05,702
Your signature needs work.

259
00:29:07,245 --> 00:29:10,123
<i>It soon came time to put our preparations
to the test.</i>

260
00:29:10,498 --> 00:29:14,252
<i>Jerome had been engineered with everything
he needed to get into Gattaca</i>

261
00:29:14,878 --> 00:29:16,588
<i>except the desire to do so.</i>

262
00:29:17,339 --> 00:29:19,039
Do you really want to be in there?

263
00:29:19,299 --> 00:29:22,594
I don't wanna be in there.
I wanna be up there.

264
00:29:25,138 --> 00:29:29,434
- What's up there?
- That's what I want to find out, Jerome.

265
00:29:31,853 --> 00:29:35,732
Call me Eugene. My middle name.

266
00:29:37,901 --> 00:29:41,151
If you're going to be Jerome,
you better start getting used to it.

267
00:29:51,706 --> 00:29:55,656
- I gotta get going. Where's my sample?
- In the refrigerator. Door on the left.

268
00:29:57,796 --> 00:30:00,173
- Which pouch?
- Any of them.

269
00:30:03,927 --> 00:30:05,470
You think I should test it?

270
00:30:06,471 --> 00:30:07,555
If you like.

271
00:30:16,272 --> 00:30:19,275
- What's wrong with the machine?
- There's nothing wrong with the machine.

272
00:30:19,359 --> 00:30:21,277
It's a hot sample!
You've been drinking again.

273
00:30:21,361 --> 00:30:22,987
- I haven't.
- Oh, Christ, Eugene.

274
00:30:23,071 --> 00:30:24,697
It's eight o'clock in the morning!

275
00:30:24,781 --> 00:30:25,865
I haven't.

276
00:30:33,289 --> 00:30:36,418
This is no joke.
My interview is in an hour!

277
00:30:42,882 --> 00:30:46,135
There is more vodka in this piss
than there is piss.

278
00:30:46,219 --> 00:30:49,013
All right, I'm sorry.
I had half a glass to celebrate.

279
00:30:49,097 --> 00:30:49,973
I'm sorry.

280
00:30:50,306 --> 00:30:52,142
Try Friday's, that one will be okay.

281
00:30:53,018 --> 00:30:54,644
There's only Wednesday left.

282
00:30:54,728 --> 00:30:56,604
Wednesday. That's the one.

283
00:31:08,992 --> 00:31:12,120
Listen, it's not too late to back out.

284
00:31:15,957 --> 00:31:19,207
This is the last day that you're
gonna be you and I'm gonna be me.

285
00:31:23,590 --> 00:31:24,424
Well?

286
00:31:54,370 --> 00:31:55,455
Congratulations.

287
00:31:57,373 --> 00:31:59,959
Well, what about the interview?

288
00:32:00,877 --> 00:32:01,961
That was it.

289
00:32:10,970 --> 00:32:11,805
I got it.

290
00:32:14,057 --> 00:32:15,350
Of course you got it.

291
00:32:25,860 --> 00:32:27,320
<i>And that's the way it was.</i>

292
00:32:27,987 --> 00:32:30,740
<i>Each day I would dispose of
as much loose skin,</i>

293
00:32:30,824 --> 00:32:32,908
<i>fingernails, and hair, as possible</i>

294
00:32:32,992 --> 00:32:38,540
<i>to limit how much of my invalid self
I would leave in the valid world.</i>

295
00:32:44,796 --> 00:32:49,175
<i>At the same time, Eugene prepared samples
of his own superior body matter</i>

296
00:32:49,259 --> 00:32:50,885
<i>so that I might pass for him.</i>

297
00:32:51,344 --> 00:32:54,722
<i>Customized urine pouches
for the frequent substance tests,</i>

298
00:32:55,473 --> 00:32:58,684
<i>fingertip blood sachets
for security checks,</i>

299
00:32:58,768 --> 00:33:01,521
<i>and vials filled with other traces.</i>

300
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:05,066
<i>While Eugene supplied me
with a new identity,</i>

301
00:33:05,150 --> 00:33:09,487
<i>I paid the rent and kept him in the style
to which he'd become accustomed.</i>

302
00:33:10,572 --> 00:33:13,407
<i>Eugene never suffered
from the routine discrimination</i>

303
00:33:13,491 --> 00:33:17,203
<i>of a "utero, " a "faith birth,"
or "invalid" as we were called.</i>

304
00:33:17,287 --> 00:33:22,417
<i>A "valid, " a "vitro, " a made man,
he suffered under a different burden:</i>

305
00:33:23,001 --> 00:33:24,419
<i>The burden of perfection.</i>

306
00:33:26,546 --> 00:33:30,257
<i>I was now a member of a relatively new
and particularly detested</i>

307
00:33:30,341 --> 00:33:31,843
<i>segment of society.</i>

308
00:33:32,427 --> 00:33:35,430
<i>One of those who refuses
to play the hand that he was dealt.</i>

309
00:33:35,930 --> 00:33:38,766
<i>I am most commonly known
as a "borrowed ladder."</i>

310
00:33:39,434 --> 00:33:42,187
<i>Or... a "de-gene-erate."</i>

311
00:33:48,693 --> 00:33:50,444
<i>In the guise of Jerome Morrow,</i>

312
00:33:50,528 --> 00:33:53,239
<i>I've risen quickly
through the ranks of Gattaca.</i>

313
00:33:53,823 --> 00:33:55,366
<i>Only one of the mission directors</i>

314
00:33:55,450 --> 00:33:58,411
<i>has ever come close
to discovering my true identity.</i>

315
00:33:59,204 --> 00:34:03,082
<i>It's strange to think he may have
more success exposing me in death</i>

316
00:34:03,166 --> 00:34:05,460
<i>than he ever did in life.</i>

317
00:34:12,926 --> 00:34:15,469
<i>Level 8 is temporarily closed.</i>

318
00:34:15,553 --> 00:34:17,763
<i>Please evacuate immediately.</i>

319
00:34:17,847 --> 00:34:20,183
<i>We apologize for any inconvenience.</i>

320
00:34:27,774 --> 00:34:31,110
- Well, I think we can rule out suicide.
- Excuse me, sir?

321
00:34:31,486 --> 00:34:32,362
Nothing.

322
00:34:34,072 --> 00:34:35,114
Thank you.

323
00:34:38,284 --> 00:34:41,454
This is going to take a while.
You may have to push things back.

324
00:34:55,635 --> 00:34:59,973
Irene, I want you
to assist the investigators.

325
00:35:00,515 --> 00:35:02,808
My work, director.
That would put me behind.

326
00:35:02,892 --> 00:35:05,395
I appreciate your sacrifice, Irene.

327
00:35:06,688 --> 00:35:09,106
Will you see to it
I don't lose my place in line?

328
00:35:09,190 --> 00:35:11,067
Your place is assured.

329
00:35:11,651 --> 00:35:17,282
So, will you kindly inform the authorities
that we will cooperate in any way,

330
00:35:17,782 --> 00:35:20,868
although we can't tolerate
a major disruption.

331
00:35:20,952 --> 00:35:23,162
- Director.
- Thank you, Irene.

332
00:35:23,788 --> 00:35:24,914
Thank you, director.

333
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:30,253
Director, how will this affect
the mission?

334
00:35:30,920 --> 00:35:33,506
Oh, we're still going ahead as planned.

335
00:35:34,090 --> 00:35:37,009
The launch window is only open
until week's end,

336
00:35:37,093 --> 00:35:40,346
and tragic though this event may be,

337
00:35:40,972 --> 00:35:44,934
it hasn't stopped the planets turning,
has it?

338
00:35:55,987 --> 00:35:58,573
- What happened?
- I'm going up.

339
00:35:59,198 --> 00:36:00,116
When?

340
00:36:01,326 --> 00:36:05,747
- End of the week.
- End of the week? That soon?

341
00:36:07,290 --> 00:36:08,958
What about the mission director?

342
00:36:11,794 --> 00:36:12,920
He's dead.

343
00:36:15,256 --> 00:36:16,341
Are you serious?

344
00:36:17,925 --> 00:36:19,760
They found him in his office this morning,

345
00:36:19,844 --> 00:36:22,221
beaten so badly
they had to check his nametag.

346
00:36:24,766 --> 00:36:25,933
You are serious.

347
00:36:27,226 --> 00:36:31,689
So, that's it.
There's nothing between us and ignition.

348
00:36:34,359 --> 00:36:35,485
I'm going up.

349
00:36:37,695 --> 00:36:40,281
- The man was murdered?
- So? I didn't do it.

350
00:36:40,365 --> 00:36:43,701
I didn't say you did, but the place
is gonna be crawling with Hoovers.

351
00:36:43,785 --> 00:36:47,035
I'll take my chances with the J. Edgars
over the mission director.

352
00:36:47,372 --> 00:36:49,373
Besides, what if they do find something?

353
00:36:49,457 --> 00:36:51,807
I'm gonna be slightly
out of their jurisdiction.

354
00:36:58,049 --> 00:36:59,699
We have to get drunk immediately.

355
00:37:07,600 --> 00:37:09,184
- Good evening, gentlemen.
- Good evening.

356
00:37:09,268 --> 00:37:10,185
My table's ready?

357
00:37:10,269 --> 00:37:12,062
I understand you're leaving us,
Mr. Morrow.

358
00:37:12,146 --> 00:37:14,649
- Yeah, looks that way.
- We're going to miss you.

359
00:37:14,899 --> 00:37:17,399
- You've opened the wine, I hope?
- Of course, sir.

360
00:37:18,111 --> 00:37:20,711
- Pour yourself a glass, Cavendish.
- Thank you, sir.

361
00:37:44,512 --> 00:37:46,389
- How old?
- Me?

362
00:37:46,722 --> 00:37:47,681
The specimen.

363
00:37:47,765 --> 00:37:50,810
I kissed him five minutes ago.
A real good one.

364
00:37:51,894 --> 00:37:53,396
I'll see what I can do.

365
00:37:58,317 --> 00:38:02,238
- You want the full sequence?
- Yes. Thank you.

366
00:38:03,614 --> 00:38:04,615
Good luck.

367
00:38:15,042 --> 00:38:18,754
[woman 2 over PA] <i>Some samples may be
consumed in the sequencing process.</i>

368
00:38:22,633 --> 00:38:25,261
Nine-point-three. Quite a catch.

369
00:38:26,721 --> 00:38:29,390
Yes. Quite a catch.

370
00:38:56,417 --> 00:38:58,669
God, how many times have I told Cavendish?

371
00:38:59,629 --> 00:39:02,464
This can't have been opened
more than five minutes ago.

372
00:39:02,548 --> 00:39:05,342
A wine like this needs to breathe.
Remember that.

373
00:39:05,426 --> 00:39:08,262
You know, they say when you're weightless,

374
00:39:09,305 --> 00:39:11,682
it's the closest thing
to being in the womb.

375
00:39:15,603 --> 00:39:19,899
I can't believe you pulled this off.
They're sending you up there.

376
00:39:21,108 --> 00:39:23,485
Up there, for Christ's sake.
You, of all people.

377
00:39:23,569 --> 00:39:25,821
I hate to think of you alone
in that room for a year.

378
00:39:25,905 --> 00:39:27,072
Well, don't think about it.

379
00:39:27,156 --> 00:39:31,118
Anyway... the room I'm stuck in
is bigger than your tin can.

380
00:39:31,202 --> 00:39:33,702
You know what I'm saying.
What are you going to do?

381
00:39:34,539 --> 00:39:37,375
I have my books. I go places in my head.

382
00:39:38,668 --> 00:39:41,128
- I'd be happier if you had some company.
- I have visitors.

383
00:39:41,212 --> 00:39:43,172
Yeah, well, none that you don't pay.

384
00:39:43,256 --> 00:39:47,969
- I wouldn't have it any other way.
- Seriously, what are you going to do?

385
00:39:50,805 --> 00:39:52,055
I'm going to finish this.

386
00:40:05,903 --> 00:40:07,780
What's Titan like this time of year?

387
00:40:11,158 --> 00:40:15,705
What's Titan like?
Titan is exactly like this.

388
00:40:27,967 --> 00:40:31,803
All the time.
It's got a cloud around it so thick,

389
00:40:31,887 --> 00:40:33,639
nobody can tell what's underneath.

390
00:40:35,266 --> 00:40:36,666
Maybe there's nothing there.

391
00:40:37,852 --> 00:40:39,061
There's something there.

392
00:40:44,859 --> 00:40:46,569
You should be going instead of me.

393
00:40:48,988 --> 00:40:49,947
Why is that?

394
00:40:51,532 --> 00:40:53,701
Because up there,
your legs wouldn't matter.

395
00:41:01,125 --> 00:41:02,376
I'm scared of heights.

396
00:41:23,689 --> 00:41:24,857
I'm sorry.

397
00:41:26,984 --> 00:41:30,363
Do you want this?
I'll save some for you, shall I?

398
00:41:40,039 --> 00:41:42,889
- I'm so sorry. I'm really sorry.
- You're not that sorry.

399
00:41:43,084 --> 00:41:44,335
I'm really, really sorry.

400
00:41:48,714 --> 00:41:51,300
Don't give me a hard time.
Give me a break.

401
00:41:54,428 --> 00:41:59,517
- Come on! Come on! Help! Help!
- I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

402
00:42:09,193 --> 00:42:12,195
- You know, I wasn't drunk.
- What do you mean, you're not drunk?

403
00:42:12,279 --> 00:42:15,533
- When I walked in front of that car.
- What car?

404
00:42:16,659 --> 00:42:18,452
I stepped right out in front of it.

405
00:42:20,204 --> 00:42:22,248
I'd never been more sober in my life.

406
00:42:25,376 --> 00:42:28,337
- Go to sleep.
- I couldn't even get that right, could I?

407
00:42:29,630 --> 00:42:33,718
If at first you don't succeed,
try, try again.

408
00:42:34,343 --> 00:42:35,386
Go to sleep.

409
00:42:41,517 --> 00:42:43,227
I'm proud of you, Vincent.

410
00:42:47,690 --> 00:42:49,817
You must be drunk to call me Vincent.

411
00:43:30,775 --> 00:43:32,985
- How much do you need?
- Twenty minutes.

412
00:43:54,965 --> 00:43:56,091
You can go.

413
00:44:03,182 --> 00:44:05,082
It's all right, Irene. You're excused.

414
00:44:29,917 --> 00:44:30,960
We found our man.

415
00:44:31,418 --> 00:44:34,713
It's the only specimen in the entire place
that can't be accounted for.

416
00:44:34,797 --> 00:44:37,675
- Every ten years or so, things go our way.
- The eyelash?

417
00:44:38,592 --> 00:44:41,542
- What do you know about it?
- It's an unregistered invalid.

418
00:44:41,720 --> 00:44:45,015
He was a janitor here until a few years
back when he disappeared out of sight.

419
00:44:45,099 --> 00:44:47,350
So, the specimen
could have been here since then.

420
00:44:47,434 --> 00:44:48,894
Well, take a look at the floor.

421
00:44:48,978 --> 00:44:51,354
I don't think anything lays around here
for very long.

422
00:44:51,438 --> 00:44:53,815
There's also motive.
Is a janitor gonna come back here

423
00:44:53,899 --> 00:44:56,026
after all this time
to kill a man he never knew?

424
00:44:56,110 --> 00:44:58,710
Well, his profile does suggest
a violent temperament.

425
00:44:59,196 --> 00:45:02,783
If I'm not mistaken, his profile
also suggests he's a sick man.

426
00:45:02,867 --> 00:45:05,118
There's a 90 percent chance
the owner of this eyelash

427
00:45:05,202 --> 00:45:07,120
has already died himself
of natural causes.

428
00:45:07,204 --> 00:45:09,539
That means there's a 10 percent chance
he's still alive.

429
00:45:09,623 --> 00:45:11,082
It's a lost-and-found case.

430
00:45:11,166 --> 00:45:14,753
All we have to do is find the man who's
minus an eyelash, we have the murderer.

431
00:45:14,837 --> 00:45:17,088
I'll do a cross-check,
find out about his family...

432
00:45:17,172 --> 00:45:20,259
I've already run it.
There's no record of any living relative.

433
00:45:20,634 --> 00:45:22,260
Well, that's a damn shame, sir.

434
00:45:22,344 --> 00:45:26,223
I want you to check the entry log.
Alibis, grudges.

435
00:45:26,307 --> 00:45:28,225
I look around, I see a lot of dry eyes.

436
00:45:28,309 --> 00:45:31,979
The mission director
was not universally loved.

437
00:45:32,605 --> 00:45:34,607
He was leading cutbacks in the program.

438
00:45:36,650 --> 00:45:38,050
With all due respect, sir...

439
00:45:42,323 --> 00:45:43,407
Whatever you say.

440
00:45:59,924 --> 00:46:02,301
Jerome, Jerome, the metronome.

441
00:46:02,760 --> 00:46:05,110
I could play the piano
by that heartbeat of his.

442
00:46:07,514 --> 00:46:10,809
Mr. Director, you will be pleased to know
that we have a suspect.

443
00:46:10,893 --> 00:46:13,019
That is a relief. Who is it?

444
00:46:13,103 --> 00:46:17,232
We found an unaccounted-for specimen
in the south wing, and here's his picture.

445
00:46:19,360 --> 00:46:22,738
- An invalid.
- We are still following other leads.

446
00:46:23,280 --> 00:46:26,741
Be that as it may,
I'm having this posted immediately.

447
00:46:26,825 --> 00:46:27,925
I'm curious, director.

448
00:46:28,619 --> 00:46:33,040
An advantage, I should imagine,
in your line of work.

449
00:46:34,166 --> 00:46:38,462
- Your hiring practices...
- Our recruitment philosophy.

450
00:46:39,088 --> 00:46:40,839
Who do you have to be to be here?

451
00:46:41,548 --> 00:46:45,886
Well, naturally, our standard
is beyond that of the common citizen.

452
00:46:45,970 --> 00:46:49,222
Even amongst your people, you must
have varying levels of excellence?

453
00:46:49,306 --> 00:46:53,519
Occasionally we've been forced to accept
candidates with minor shortcomings.

454
00:46:54,019 --> 00:46:57,480
But nothing that would prohibit someone
from working in a field

455
00:46:57,564 --> 00:47:00,484
such as law enforcement, for example.

456
00:47:00,943 --> 00:47:04,821
But now there are enough
of the right kind of people

457
00:47:04,905 --> 00:47:07,366
to warrant a new measuring stick.

458
00:47:07,783 --> 00:47:09,743
Bodies with minds to match.

459
00:47:09,827 --> 00:47:14,665
Essential as we push out
further and further.

460
00:47:14,999 --> 00:47:17,042
Yet you still closely monitor performance.

461
00:47:17,126 --> 00:47:20,337
We have to ensure that people
are meeting their potential.

462
